### What is the Remove Duplicates problem?

Given a sorted array or sorted linked list, remove duplicate elements in place so each element appears only once, and return the new length (array) or the de-duplicated list. No extra structure may be allocated. Because the data is sorted, every group of equal values is contiguous, so duplicates are always neighbours.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class DuplicateRemover {
    // Returns the count of unique elements
    public int removeDuplicates(int[] sorted) {
        // implementation below
        return 0;
    }
}
```

### How does the slow-fast pointer approach work for arrays?

Keep a `slow` index at position 0, pointing to the last unique element. Scan with a `fast` index from 1 to the end. If `arr[fast]` differs from `arr[slow]`, increment slow and copy `arr[fast]` into `arr[slow]`. If they are equal, skip. After the scan, positions 0 through slow hold the unique values, and the unique count is `slow + 1`.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class AccountDeduplicator {
    public static int removeDuplicates(int[] accounts) {
        if (accounts.length == 0) return 0;
        int slow = 0;
        for (int fast = 1; fast < accounts.length; fast++) {
            if (accounts[fast] != accounts[slow]) {
                slow++;
                accounts[slow] = accounts[fast];
            }
        }
        return slow + 1;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] accounts = {101, 101, 102, 103, 103, 103, 104};
        int uniqueCount = removeDuplicates(accounts);
        System.out.print("Unique accounts: ");
        for (int i = 0; i < uniqueCount; i++) {
            System.out.print(accounts[i] + " ");
        }
        System.out.println("(count: " + uniqueCount + ")");
    }
}
```

```text
Unique accounts: 101 102 103 104 (count: 4)
```

### How does the linked list version work?

Walk a `current` pointer through the sorted list. While `current.next` exists and has the same value as `current`, bypass the duplicate by setting `current.next = current.next.next`. Otherwise advance `current`. This removes all consecutive duplicates in one pass.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class SortedListDeduplicator {
    static class ListNode {
        int val;
        ListNode next;
        ListNode(int val) { this.val = val; }
    }

    public static ListNode removeDuplicates(ListNode head) {
        ListNode current = head;
        while (current != null && current.next != null) {
            if (current.val == current.next.val) {
                current.next = current.next.next; // unlink duplicate
            } else {
                current = current.next;
            }
        }
        return head;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ListNode head = new ListNode(101);
        head.next = new ListNode(101);
        head.next.next = new ListNode(102);
        head.next.next.next = new ListNode(103);
        head.next.next.next.next = new ListNode(103);

        ListNode result = removeDuplicates(head);
        while (result != null) {
            System.out.print(result.val + " ");
            result = result.next;
        }
    }
}
```

```text
101 102 103
```

### What is the "allow at most two" variant?

A common variant allows each element to appear at most twice. For the array version, compare `arr[fast]` against `arr[slow - 1]` instead of `arr[slow]`. If they differ, the value has appeared fewer than twice, so copy it.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class AllowTwoDuplicates {
    public static int removeDuplicates(int[] nums) {
        if (nums.length <= 2) return nums.length;
        int slow = 1;
        for (int fast = 2; fast < nums.length; fast++) {
            if (nums[fast] != nums[slow - 1]) {
                slow++;
                nums[slow] = nums[fast];
            }
        }
        return slow + 1;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] nums = {101, 101, 101, 102, 102, 103};
        int len = removeDuplicates(nums);
        for (int i = 0; i < len; i++) System.out.print(nums[i] + " ");
    }
}
```

```text
101 101 102 102 103
```

### What about the harder linked list variant?

The harder variant removes all nodes that ever had a duplicate. A dummy head is needed because even the original head might be removed. Walk with a `prev` pointer; when `prev.next` and `prev.next.next` have the same value, skip the entire run of that value.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class RemoveAllDuplicates {
    static class ListNode {
        int val;
        ListNode next;
        ListNode(int val) { this.val = val; }
    }

    public static ListNode deleteDuplicates(ListNode head) {
        ListNode dummy = new ListNode(0);
        dummy.next = head;
        ListNode prev = dummy;
        while (prev.next != null && prev.next.next != null) {
            if (prev.next.val == prev.next.next.val) {
                int dupVal = prev.next.val;
                while (prev.next != null && prev.next.val == dupVal) {
                    prev.next = prev.next.next;
                }
            } else {
                prev = prev.next;
            }
        }
        return dummy.next;
    }
}
```

### What are edge cases?

Edge cases include an empty array (return 0), a single element (return 1), and an array where all elements are the same (return 1). For the linked list, an empty list returns null and a single-node list returns the node unchanged.

```java
removeDuplicates(new int[]{});          // 0
removeDuplicates(new int[]{42});        // 1
removeDuplicates(new int[]{5, 5, 5});   // 1
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is returning `slow` instead of `slow + 1` for the array version, which undercounts by one. Another is advancing `current` inside the if-branch of the linked list version, which skips the comparison of the new next node.

```java
// Wrong: advance current after unlinking
if (current.val == current.next.val) {
    current.next = current.next.next;
    current = current.next; // BUG: skips comparison
}

// Correct: only advance in the else branch
if (current.val == current.next.val) {
    current.next = current.next.next; // stay at current
} else {
    current = current.next;
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer knows the three variants (unique only, allow two, remove all duplicated nodes), which comparison to use for each, and when a dummy head is needed. They know the array method cannot physically shrink the array in Java, so the logical length is returned and the tail is ignored. They handle the comparison correctly for both primitive and object types.
